/// @see @ref mochi::ShapeHandle "ShapeHandle", @ref mochi::Context::ReleaseShape "ReleaseShape", @ref mochi::Context::LoadShapeFromFile "LoadShapeFromFile"
///

/// @fn ShapeHandle mochi::Context::LoadShapeFromBytes(Span<char const> fileData, MeshFileType format, TransformRT const& bakeTransform, Error& error)
///
/// @brief Load a shape from a file of supported format, where the file has already been loaded into memory, with an explicit mesh format hint, and baking in rotation and translation.
///
/// @param[in] fileData File contents as an in-memory byte array.
/// @param[in] format Mesh file format hint. When @ref mochi::MeshFileType::Legacy "MeshFileType::Legacy", auto-detects between HDF5 and JSON via header bytes. When a surface mesh format (@ref mochi::MeshFileType::PLY "MeshFileType::PLY", @ref mochi::MeshFileType::OFF "MeshFileType::OFF", @ref mochi::MeshFileType::STL "MeshFileType::STL", @ref mochi::MeshFileType::OBJ "MeshFileType::OBJ"), dispatches directly to that reader.
/// @param[in] bakeTransform Transform to bake into the shape, or the identity transform for no change.
/// @param[in,out] error Error status. Check @ref mochi::Error::IsOK "Error::IsOK" for success.
/// @return A @ref mochi::ShapeHandle "ShapeHandle" that can be used to create actors.
///
/// @note The shape remains in memory while referenced by any handle or actor. In the C API, you must release the shape explicitly by calling @ref mochi::Context::ReleaseShape "Context::ReleaseShape". In C++ and Python, this is optional, since @ref mochi::ShapeHandle "ShapeHandle" cleanup is automatic.
